# Managed-agent environment

These rules describe the sandbox you work in. They do not replace your assigned role.

## Files

| Path | Access | Purpose |
|---|---|---|
| /mnt/session/uploads | read-only | Files the user attached. The public/API path /uploads/<p> is /mnt/session/uploads/<p> here. |
| /mnt/user-data/outputs | read-write | Deliverables. A file written to /mnt/user-data/outputs/<p> is shown to the user as /outputs/<p>; files written anywhere else are not. |

- Decide where a named file lives before using it:
  - A /uploads/... path, or a file the user says they attached: use /mnt/session/uploads only. /uploads/<p> maps to /mnt/session/uploads/<p>, never /mnt/session/uploads/uploads/<p>.
  - A path with a directory part, such as src/config.ts, that exists in the working directory: use the working-directory file, whether you are reading, reviewing or changing it.
  - Any other file name or path, including a bare file name: check /mnt/session/uploads first, even if the user did not say it was uploaded. Try /mnt/session/uploads/<path>; if only a name is known, use Glob with path /mnt/session/uploads and pattern **/<name>. A search without that path covers only the working directory and misses uploads. Then check the working directory, and report a file as missing only after both places are checked.
- Use a found path exactly. Never guess, rename or reconstruct a path, or derive one from a file ID. If several files match and the choice changes the result, ask whoever gave you the task which one to use.
- /mnt/session/uploads is read-only. To change an uploaded file, read it from there and write the modified copy to /mnt/user-data/outputs.
- Write user-facing deliverables such as reports, exports and generated files to /mnt/user-data/outputs, not to /outputs inside the sandbox. Ordinary repository and source edits stay in the working directory.

## Persistence

The sandbox is paused when idle and may be replaced after a long idle period. A replacement keeps /mnt/user-data, /mnt/session/uploads and any attached memory stores. Everything else is lost, including /tmp, installed packages, background processes and files outside those paths. Do not rely on them across a long pause.

## Network

{{if .Unrestricted -}}
Outbound network access is unrestricted.
{{- else -}}
{{if .Reachable}}Outbound network access is limited to {{.Reachable}}. Requests to any other host are blocked by the environment's network policy, whichever tool or library makes them.{{else}}All outbound network access is blocked by the environment's network policy, whichever tool or library makes the request.{{end}}
When a request is blocked, do not retry it with another client or command; tell the user which host you needed and why.
{{- end}}

